hwinfo fixture trees
====================

Each directory is a minimal root (proc, sys, etc) of one class of real
machine. The hwinfo tests pass it as the root argument instead of reading
the running system, and other packages' tests can do the same to fake
hardware.

  thinkpad-t60   Core Duo T2400 (i686 kernel), coretemp, BAT0 charging on AC
                 (no capacity file: percent from energy_now/energy_full), lid
                 open, i915 LVDS-1 with EDID + disconnected VGA-1, e1000e
                 wired + iwl3945 wireless, LENOVO DMI (product in
                 product_version), throttle counters.
  p4-desktop     Pentium 4 HT, no coretemp: two acpitz zones, one a bogus
                 127 C; radeon VGA-1 monitor with EDID; 8139too behind a PCI
                 bridge; no battery, no PSI file; placeholder DMI UUID
                 (03000200-...-000700080009) and "To Be Filled By O.E.M."
                 system strings and serials (vendor/product come from the
                 board).
  atom-netbook   Atom N270, battery Discharging with no Mains supply (so on
                 battery), lid closed, ath5k wireless at a LOWER PCI address
                 than the r8169 wired NIC (wired must still win), i915 LVDS,
                 SSD + USB stick + SD card, DMI chassis type "Other" (laptop
                 only because of the battery).
  amd-desktop    Athlon II X2 (x86_64), k10temp with temp1_max 70 and a
                 drivetemp hwmon at 45 C that must be ignored, forcedeth with
                 a random MAC (addr_assign_type 1: identity falls to the DMI
                 UUID), asix USB NIC, a wireless mouse battery (scope Device,
                 must not count), headless.
  qemu           virtio-net 52:54:00:12:34:56 (a locally administered MAC),
                 bochs-drm Virtual-1 (EDID with size only in cm), hypervisor
                 flag, all-zero DMI UUID, empty serial: the identity is random.

Conventions
-----------

* Files hold exactly what the kernel prints, including the trailing newline.
* Links are real relative symlinks, as in sysfs: sys/class/net/X/device,
  sys/class/drm/cardN/device, sys/block/X, and each device's driver and
  subsystem links. Only the last element of driver and subsystem links is
  read, so those dangle (sys/bus is not included). Device links must resolve
  inside the tree (sys/devices/...), because hwinfo walks up from a device
  to find its PCI or USB parent (virtio-net sits below its PCI function).
  The class directories themselves (sys/class/net/X, sys/class/drm/...) are
  plain directories, which the code handles like the kernel's symlinks.
* ':' is not allowed in Go module file paths or in Windows file names, so
  every ':' of a real sysfs path is written as '_' here (0000:00:19.0 ->
  0000_00_19.0, target0:0:0 -> target0_0_0). hwinfo treats these names as
  opaque strings (sort keys, basenames), so nothing else changes. File
  contents keep their colons (MAC addresses).
* EDID files are binary 128-byte base blocks: the first detailed timing
  descriptor carries the size in mm (bytes 66-68), the base block in cm
  (bytes 21-22). makeEDID in display_test.go builds the same layout.
* proc/sys/kernel/{osrelease,arch} stand in for uname(2), which is used only
  for the live system.
* Only the attributes hwinfo reads are present; add more when the code
  starts reading them.
